    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d995">

                <pb facs="0869" n="857" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg857"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">822.51/856: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Long</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-04-25T22:00:00-05:00">April 25, 1940—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 26—9:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>31. Provisional President<anchor n="38a" xml:id="d995-f1"/> has just informed me
                    that Central Bank will probably have to announce tomorrow or next day that it
                    will temporarily suspend sale of dollar exchange to commercial banks.</p>
                <p>Dr. Cordova apprehended that this announcement would produce economic panic, at
                    least a run on sucre, with possible political disturbances of communistic
                    character. He thought that situation might be partially relieved if the
                    announcement could be made that exchange expert was to be offered to the Central
                        Bank;<anchor n="39" xml:id="d995-f2"/> that economic adviser<anchor n="40"
                        xml:id="d995-f3"/> would arrive to consult with Finance Minister; that cacao
                    experiment would be undertaken and above all an announcement that construction
                    of Pan-American Highway would be undertaken; if indeed any of these are
                    true.</p>
                <p>My letter of April 22<anchor n="41" xml:id="d995-f4"/> to Mr. Welles<anchor
                        n="42" xml:id="d995-f5"/> gives only a partial outline of present emergency.
                    Early telegraph reply will be appreciated.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Long</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d995-f1" n="38a" xml:id="d995-f1-content">
                <p>Andres F. Cordova.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d995-f2" n="39" xml:id="d995-f2-content">
                <p>In June 1940 Professor Frank Fetter was appointed technical adviser to the
                    Central Bank of Ecuador.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d995-f3" n="40" xml:id="d995-f3-content">
                <p>Mr. Harold Glasser of the Treasury Department was detailed as adviser to the
                    Ecuadoran Ministry of Finance in July 1940.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d995-f4" n="41" xml:id="d995-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d995-f5" n="42" xml:id="d995-f5-content">
                <p>Sumner Welles, Under Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
